"[2511.08972] Selective Sinkhorn Routing for Improved Sparse Mixture of Experts (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08972)\nciteturn5912view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08972\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 166\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 4 Jun 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Selective Sinkhorn Routing for Improved Sparse Mixture of Experts\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Duc Anh Nguyen , cite7†Huu Binh Ta , cite8†Nhuan Le Duc , cite9†Tan Minh Nguyen , cite10†Toan Tran L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Selective Sinkhorn Routing for Improved Sparse Mixture of Experts, by Duc Anh Nguyen and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Sparse Mixture-of-Experts (SMoE) models are scalable and computationally efficient, enabling large increases in model capacity with limited inference overhead. Existing SMoE methods often depend on auxiliary objectives, such as load-balancing loss and z-loss, or additional trainable components such as noisy gating.\nL17: While these techniques encourage expert diversity, they can introduce objective misalignment, increase model complexity, or incur substantial training overhead, especially in Sinkhorn-based routing methods. In this paper, we revisit the token-to-expert assignment as an optimal transport problem. We add constraints to ensure balanced expert utilization. We show that even minimal optimal transport-based routing improves SMoE performance without requiring auxiliary balancing losses.\nL18: Unlike prior approaches, our method derives gating scores directly from the transport map, leading to more balanced and effective token-to-expert assignments. Building on this insight, we introduce Selective Sinkhorn Routing (SSR), a lightweight routing mechanism that replaces complex auxiliary losses with efficient Sinkhorn-based routing while preserving flexible expert selection.\nL19: Experiments on language modeling and image classification show that SSR improves training efficiency, accuracy, and robustness to input corruption.\nL20: Comments:  | 12 pages, 5 figures\nL21: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2511.08972 [cs.LG]\nL23:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2511.08972v2 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL24:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.08972†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Anh Nguyen Duc [cite16†view email ]\nL28: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 04:29:05 UTC (192 KB)\nL29: [v2] Thu, 4 Jun 2026 02:54:34 UTC (201 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Selective Sinkhorn Routing for Improved Sparse Mixture of Experts, by Duc Anh Nguyen and 4 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite11†View PDF L38:   * cite12†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite17†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite18†view license†creativecommons.org L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.LG\nL45: \nL46: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L47: \nL48: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2025-11 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite24†cs L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72