rch arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 18 Nov 2025 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:LoopTool: Closing the Data-Training Loop for Robust LLM Tool Calls\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Kangning Zhang , cite7†Wenxiang Jiao , cite8†Kounianhua Du , cite9†Yuan Lu , cite10†Weiwen Liu , cite11†Weinan Zhang , cite12†Yong Yu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled LoopTool: Closing the Data-Training Loop for Robust LLM Tool Calls, by Kangning Zhang and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite13†View PDF cite14†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Augmenting Large Language Models (LLMs) with external tools enables them to execute complex, multi-step tasks. However, tool learning is hampered by the static synthetic data pipelines where data generation and model training are executed as two separate, non-interactive processes. This approach fails to adaptively focus on a model's specific weaknesses and allows noisy labels to persist, degrading training efficiency.\nL17: We introduce LoopTool, a fully automated, model-aware data evolution framework that closes this loop by tightly integrating data synthesis and model training.\nL18: LoopTool iteratively refines both the data and the model through three synergistic modules: (1) Greedy Capability Probing (GCP) diagnoses the model's mastered and failed capabilities; (2) Judgement-Guided Label Verification (JGLV) uses an open-source judge model to find and correct annotation errors, progressively purifying the dataset; and (3) Error-Driven Data Expansion (EDDE) generates new, challenging samples based on identified failures.\nL19: This closed-loop process operates within a cost-effective, open-source ecosystem, eliminating dependence on expensive closed-source APIs. Experiments show that our 8B model trained with LoopTool significantly surpasses its 32B data generator and achieves new state-of-the-art results on the BFCL-v3 and ACEBench benchmarks for its scale. Our work demonstrates that closed-loop, self-refining data pipelines can dramatically enhance the tool-use capabilities of LLMs.\nL20: Comments:  | The code is accessible at cite15†this https URL†github.com . The LoopTool-8B is accessible at cite16†this https URL†huggingface.co L21: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite17†arXiv:2511.09148 [cs.CL]\nL23:    | (or cite18†arXiv:2511.09148v2 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL24:    | cite19†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09148†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Kangning Zhang [cite20†view email ]\nL28: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 09:34:39 UTC (2,057 KB)\nL29: [v2] Tue, 18 Nov 2025 07:03:59 UTC (2,053 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled LoopTool: Closing the Data-Training Loop for Robust LLM Tool Calls, by Kangning Zhang and 6 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite13†View PDF L38:   * cite14†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite21†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite22†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.CL\nL45: \nL46: cite23†< prev |   cite24†next > L47: \nL48: cite25†new | cite26†recent | cite27†2025-11 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite28†cs L53: cite29†cs.AI L54: cite30†cs.LG L55: \nL56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * cite31†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L59:   * cite32†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L60:   * cite33†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L61: \nL62: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL63: \nL64: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL65: \nL66: [Button: ×]\nL67: \nL68: loading...\nL69: \nL70: Data provided by:\n